---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import BackLink from "../../../components/BackLink.astro";

const publishedDate = "2026-05-13";
const title = "Private AI for law firms — Zaviren";
const description =
	"The four real pains of a law firm — searching the document archive, rewriting the same filings, repeated internal questions, onboarding — and how AI that never leaves your network solves them without touching privilege.";

const faq = [
	{
		q: "Can I use ChatGPT with client documents?",
		a: "An enterprise plan gives you a contract and guarantees, but the document still leaves the firm's perimeter and goes to a third party. That means assessing professional privilege and signing a data processing agreement (GDPR art. 28). With private AI there is no transfer at all, because the document never leaves the firm's server.",
	},
	{
		q: "Does it replace a legal research database?",
		a: "No. Those databases hold published legislation and case law; this AI answers over the firm's own archive — your filings, opinions, contracts and internal precedent. They are complementary.",
	},
	{
		q: "How do I know the answer isn't made up?",
		a: "Every answer cites the document and passage it came from, with a link to the original. If it finds no support in your archive, it says so instead of filling the gap.",
	},
	{
		q: "Do we have to migrate our case files somewhere else?",
		a: "No. It indexes the shared drive, document management system or case software you already use, exactly as it is.",
	},
	{
		q: "Can everyone see every matter?",
		a: "No: read permissions are inherited from the ones you already have. If a junior has no access to a folder, the AI returns nothing from it either.",
	},
];
---

<Layout
	lang="en"
	title={title}
	description={description}
	altHref="/blog/ia-privada-despachos-abogados"
>
	<article>
		<section class="hero">
			<span class="eyebrow">Blog · Industry</span>
			<h1>Private AI for <span class="gradient">law firms</span></h1>
			<p class="subtitle">
				A firm's knowledge lives in its filings, its opinions and its
				precedent — and finding it burns billable hours. AI that runs inside
				the firm searches it for you, without a single case file leaving the
				network.
			</p>
			<div class="post-meta">
				<span class="badge">13 May 2026</span>
				<span class="badge">6 min read</span>
			</div>
			<div class="cta-row">
				<a class="cta" href="/en/contact">Tell us about your case</a>
				<a class="cta cta-secondary" href="/en/guide">Download the free guide</a>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="callout">
				<span class="eyebrow">In one sentence</span>
				<p>
					A law firm accumulates thousands of documents that already solve the
					problem in front of it today — but nobody remembers which matter they
					are in. Private AI indexes that archive and answers in plain language,
					citing the specific filing. And because the model runs on a server in
					the firm, client documents never travel to anyone's cloud: the tool
					stops being in tension with your duty of confidentiality.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="block-head">
				<span class="eyebrow">01 · Day to day</span>
				<h2>The <span class="gradient">real pains</span></h2>
				<p>None of them are technology problems. All of them cost hours.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="pain-grid">
				<div class="card">
					<span class="pain-icon">🗂️</span>
					<div>
						<h3>"We've done this before, but I can't find which matter"</h3>
						<p>A nearly identical filing has been in the archive for three years. Finding it takes longer than drafting it again.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="pain-icon">📑</span>
					<div>
						<h3>A 60-page contract that has to be reviewed today</h3>
						<p>Locating the termination clause, the confidentiality wording or one specific deadline means reading it end to end, again.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="pain-icon">🔁</span>
					<div>
						<h3>The same internal question every week</h3>
						<p>"What line do we take on this type of matter?" — the answer sits in two partners' heads, not anywhere you can look it up.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="pain-icon">👥</span>
					<div>
						<h3>Every new hire starts from zero</h3>
						<p>A new lawyer takes months to learn how the firm actually works, because none of it is written in a manual.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="block-head">
				<span class="eyebrow">02 · The fix</span>
				<h2>What AI does <span class="gradient">about those four</span></h2>
			</div>
			<div class="cap-grid">
				<div class="card">
					<span class="cap-num">01</span>
					<div>
						<h3>Searches by what documents say, not by filename</h3>
						<p>"Objections to mortgage enforcement from the last three years" returns the actual matters, even if none of them is named that way.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="cap-num">02</span>
					<div>
						<h3>Answers questions about a long contract</h3>
						<p>Ask about a clause and you get the answer with the exact paragraph quoted, instead of a summary you have to verify in full.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="cap-num">03</span>
					<div>
						<h3>Makes the firm's own approach searchable</h3>
						<p>Internal precedent, memos and prior opinions start answering on their own, without interrupting a partner.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="cap-num">04</span>
					<div>
						<h3>Shortens onboarding</h3>
						<p>New joiners ask the system how something was handled before and get the firm's real document, not a generic explanation.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="split">
				<div class="copy">
					<span class="eyebrow">03 · Why private</span>
					<h2>The case file <span class="gradient">cannot leave the firm</span></h2>
					<p>
						This is the difference that matters in a law firm, and it isn't
						about price: with a cloud tool, every query containing client
						documents travels to a third party's server. Even with an enterprise
						plan and a data processing agreement (GDPR art. 28), there is still
						a transfer to weigh against your duty of confidentiality.
					</p>
					<p>
						With <a href="/en/private-ai">private AI</a> that transfer simply
						doesn't happen: the model and the search engine run on a server in
						the firm, connected only to the internal network. Nothing leaves,
						and the system keeps working even if the connection drops.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="illustration">
					<img src="/images/secure-server.svg" alt="A protected server inside the law firm" width="400" height="262" />
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="block-head">
				<span class="eyebrow">04 · Limits</span>
				<h2>What it does <span class="gradient">not</span> do</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					It gives no legal opinion and signs nothing: it is a search and
					drafting aid over your own documents, and a lawyer's review remains
					mandatory. It does not replace a legislation and case law database —
					it answers over the firm's archive, not over the official gazette. And
					it cannot guess what was never indexed: if an approach was never
					written down, there is nothing to retrieve.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="block-head">
				<span class="eyebrow">05 · FAQ</span>
				<h2>Before you <span class="gradient">ask</span></h2>
			</div>
			<div class="faq-list">
				{faq.map((item) => (
					<details class="faq-item">
						<summary>{item.q}</summary>
						<p>{item.a}</p>
					</details>
				))}
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="final-cta">
				<div>
					<h2>Let's look at your archive</h2>
					<p>A 30-minute call to work out which matters are worth indexing first.</p>
				</div>
				<a class="cta" href="/en/contact">Book a call</a>
			</div>
		</section>

		<div class="post-footer-links">
			<BackLink lang="en" />
			<a class="back-link" href="/en/rag-for-business">How RAG works under the hood →</a>
		</div>
	</article>
</Layout>

<style>
	.post-meta {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.6rem;
		margin-top: 1.2rem;
	}
	.post-meta .badge {
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.callout {
		max-width: 720px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		border-left: 3px solid var(--blue);
		background: var(--card);
		border-radius: 16px;
		padding: 1.6rem 1.8rem;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.6rem;
	}
	.callout p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
	}
	.prose {
		max-width: 720px;
		margin: 0 auto;
	}
	.prose p {
		margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
	}
	.prose p:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}
	.prose a,
	.copy a {
		color: inherit;
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-color: var(--border);
		text-underline-offset: 3px;
	}
	.prose a:hover,
	.copy a:hover {
		text-decoration-color: currentColor;
	}
	.post-footer-links {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: space-between;
		align-items: center;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.5rem;
	}
</style>

<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
	"@context": "https://schema.org",
	"@type": "BlogPosting",
	headline: "Private AI for law firms",
	description,
	datePublished: publishedDate,
	dateModified: publishedDate,
	author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Zaviren" },
	publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Zaviren" },
	mainEntityOfPage: "https://zaviren.com/en/blog/private-ai-law-firms",
})} />

<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
	"@context": "https://schema.org",
	"@type": "FAQPage",
	mainEntity: faq.map((item) => ({
		"@type": "Question",
		name: item.q,
		acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.a },
	})),
})} />
